.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_CH 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_ch \- declare and create a hash map that hashes its keys with a custom procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_ch(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_hashing_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name, along with the companion type
variables that pass key_t and val_t on to every later type-inferred
macro, and creates a map that hashes its keys with custom_hashing_proc
instead of the map's own hashing for key_t.

It is chmap_construct(3) with a hashing procedure added, and it uses the
default allocator. chmap_construct_mp(3) swaps the allocator instead,
chmap_construct_full(3) takes both, and chmap_construct_ch_scoped(3) is
this macro with a cleanup attribute that destroys the map at scope exit.

A custom procedure only changes how a key is turned into a number. The
implementation that the map picks from key_t and val_t stays the same,
and so does the set of keys the map considers equal.

The two-step form is chmap_declare(3) followed by chmap_init_ch(3). Use
it when a creation failure has to be handled instead of being fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name              Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key
                     and, with val_t, which implementation strategy the map
                     uses
val_t                Value type
custom_hashing_proc  A ccol_hashing_proc_t, or NULL to use the map's own
                     hashing for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro declares variables and is not an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM HASHING"
.nf
A custom procedure gets a pointer to the key bytes and their length and
returns an unsigned long. It must return the same value for any two keys
that the map considers equal, and for a char * key equal means equal
contents, not an equal pointer. For a fixed-width key type the length is
the map's fixed key width, and for a string or other buffer-like key it
is the byte length the caller gave. So the procedure can always hash
exactly that many bytes and needs no NUL terminator or length prefix of
its own.

The result doesn't have to avalanche. The map runs whatever a custom
procedure returns through its own bit-mixing step before reducing it to
a slot or a bucket, so a procedure that keeps all of its entropy in the
low bits, like a sum, a small identifier or a truncated checksum, still
spreads across the table. The only contract is "any function of the key
that respects the map's notion of key equality", with no rule about
which end of the result carries the entropy.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through _ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
Uses the default allocator and CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16)
buckets
A float or double key is canonicalized before the procedure sees it, so
a custom procedure never gets a raw -0.0
A long double key is the one case where a custom procedure changes how
keys are handled; chmap_create_full(3) says what such a procedure has to
do
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the map. The companion type variables live in
the scope of the handle, so a function that receives the handle needs
chmap_redeclare(3) inside it
An array key or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error, and the message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
a key or a value by assignment and C cannot assign an array, so the map
would store the address of the caller's array instead of its elements. A
struct that holds the array works
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
unsigned long hash_bytes(const void *p, size_t size);

chmap_construct_ch(scores, char *, int, hash_bytes);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct (3),
